-- title:
--   Theorem 4.1 — Nonvanishing strictly below the cutoff
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a characteristic-zero field and $n,r\in\mathbb N$. Let $0\ne f\in F[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$ and assume the $F$-linear span $V_f$ of all ordinary iterated partial derivatives, including order zero, has dimension at most $r$. For $1\le i\le n$, use the formal series
--   $$
--    P_i(t)=\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{(-1)^{k-1}i^k}{k}t^k=\log(1+it),\qquad B=nr^2+r.
--   $$
--   Then
--   $$
--    \exists k<B,\qquad [t^k]f(P_1(t),\ldots,P_n(t))\ne0.
--   $$
--   This is precisely nonvanishing modulo $t^B$, and it supplies the quantitative truncation needed for the finite polynomial generator. There is no degree or homogeneity restriction on $f$. Nonzero constants are included; the hypotheses have no instance when $r=0$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: variables indexed by 1,…,n → variables indexed by Fin n, with integer parameter i.val+1; reason: this is a bijective reindexing and the first kernel is nonzero
--   - encoding: dimension of the span of all ordinary partial derivatives → cardinal Module.rank of the span of derivatives indexed by all finite sequences, including the empty sequence; reason: finite-dimensional cardinal rank is ordinary dimension, and no finrank default is used
--   - encoding: nonzero modulo t^B → some coefficient of index k strictly less than nr²+r is nonzero; reason: this is exactly nonzero image in the quotient by the ideal generated by t^B
-- source:
--   Shubham Bhardwaj, Foram Lakhani, Partha Mukhopadhyay, Ramprasad Saptharishi, Pratik Shastri, Amit Sinhababu, STOC 2027 submission #577 (October 2026), p. 15, Theorem 4.1; https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/577.pdf

import Definitions.Def_DerivationHitting_Commutative_PolynomialData

namespace DerivationHitting.Commutative
theorem logarithm_order_bound {F : Type*} [Field F] [CharZero F] {n : ℕ}
    (r : ℕ) (f : MvPolynomial (Fin n) F) (hf : f ≠ 0)
    (hr : hasBoundedPartials f r) :
    ∃ k : ℕ, k < cutoff n r ∧
      PowerSeries.coeff k (logarithmSubstitution n f) ≠ 0 := by sorry
end DerivationHitting.Commutative
